1
00:02:00,977 --> 00:02:05,987
Spice and Wolf II

2
00:02:06,677 --> 00:02:12,817
When I think about it, I can't help but believe that it was God who brought us together.

3
00:02:12,817 --> 00:02:17,747
If not for you, I certainly wouldn't be sitting here drinking with a smile on my face.

4
00:02:17,747 --> 00:02:21,287
I'll always be grateful to you too, Lawrence.

5
00:02:21,917 --> 00:02:23,247
I'm glad we met.

6
00:02:23,247 --> 00:02:24,817
No, the pleasure is all mine.

7
00:02:24,817 --> 00:02:26,327
No, it's mine.

8
00:02:26,327 --> 00:02:31,377
I've even asked you to help me smuggle gold.

9
00:02:31,377 --> 00:02:35,567
To be frank, I didn't expect you to accept.

10
00:02:35,567 --> 00:02:39,527
I was surprised I accepted myself, actually.

11
00:02:40,907 --> 00:02:43,227
I sound strange, don't I?

12
00:02:48,227 --> 00:02:52,837
But somehow, in my heart, I knew all along that one day I'd have to do something bold.

13
00:02:52,837 --> 00:02:56,257
That I couldn't live my entire life under the protection of the Church.

14
00:02:56,257 --> 00:02:59,807
That's why you're my savior, Lawrence.

15
00:03:00,557 --> 00:03:02,527
I'm nothing of the sort.

16
00:03:03,347 --> 00:03:05,647
Merchants are selfish creatures.

17
00:03:05,647 --> 00:03:08,957
Well then, I suppose I am as well.

18
00:03:12,367 --> 00:03:14,077
Drinking alone, Holo?

19
00:03:14,077 --> 00:03:15,207
Have no fear.

20
00:03:15,207 --> 00:03:16,697
I'm doing fine on my own.

21
00:03:16,697 --> 00:03:18,967
Why don't you join our conversation?

22
00:03:23,957 --> 00:03:26,197
What, are you already drunk?

23
00:03:26,197 --> 00:03:29,107
I'm a wise wolf, said to have drunk an entire lake dry.

24
00:03:29,107 --> 00:03:31,787
It'll take much more than a few cups to affect me.

25
00:03:31,787 --> 00:03:32,747
All right.

26
00:03:33,437 --> 00:03:34,407
You heard her.

27
00:03:34,407 --> 00:03:36,637
I guess we'll leave her be for now.

28
00:03:36,637 --> 00:03:37,727
Are you sure?

29
00:03:37,727 --> 00:03:40,957
I'll join you after I've had a few more drinks.

30
00:03:40,957 --> 00:03:42,307
You're on your own until then.

31
00:03:42,307 --> 00:03:43,827
All right, we'll wait for you.

32
00:03:43,827 --> 00:03:46,127
So, what were we talking about?

33
00:03:46,927 --> 00:03:47,877
Ah, finally!

34
00:03:48,287 --> 00:03:50,027
It looks delicious!

35
00:03:50,757 --> 00:03:52,827
Thank you.

36
00:03:52,827 --> 00:03:53,757
Would you like some?

37
00:03:54,227 --> 00:03:56,217
Thanks, but I can serve myself.

38
00:03:56,217 --> 00:03:57,807
You're supposed to be leaving me alone, remember?

39
00:03:57,807 --> 00:03:58,927
Right.

40
00:03:59,297 --> 00:04:01,037
What were we talking about?

41
00:04:01,037 --> 00:04:04,597
Hmm, wasn't it about how we first met?

42
00:04:04,597 --> 00:04:06,487
What about before that?

43
00:04:06,967 --> 00:04:09,497
Oh! I think you mean the sheep and rock salt.

44
00:04:09,497 --> 00:04:10,917
Yeah, that was it.

45
00:04:11,527 --> 00:04:14,907
You know, I had no idea that sheep could be used to find rock salt.

46
00:04:15,357 --> 00:04:16,537
It's true.

47
00:04:16,537 --> 00:04:19,267
Sheep are fond of salt.

48
00:04:19,787 --> 00:04:23,187
For example, if I were to put salt on a rock,

49
00:04:23,187 --> 00:04:25,767
they would lick it endlessly.

50
00:04:26,217 --> 00:04:28,387
So the story I heard might be true.

51
00:04:28,387 --> 00:04:29,657
What story is that?

52
00:04:29,657 --> 00:04:34,707
I've heard sheep are used for torture in a city to the east.

53
00:04:34,707 --> 00:04:36,757
I didn't believe it then, but now...

54
00:04:37,107 --> 00:04:39,027
Using sheep for torture?

55
00:04:39,027 --> 00:04:40,097
Indeed.

56
00:04:40,097 --> 00:04:41,707
It's quite an ingenious technique.

57
00:04:41,707 --> 00:04:46,807
They tie the criminals up, put salt on their feet, and then bring the sheep in.

58
00:04:48,447 --> 00:04:50,397
Salt on their feet, huh?

59
00:04:57,097 --> 00:04:59,817
You will soon taste my wrath.

60
00:04:59,817 --> 00:05:00,947
Sorry.

61
00:05:00,947 --> 00:05:03,647
But if they put salt on their feet, then the sheep would...

62
00:05:03,647 --> 00:05:06,267
They'd lick them enthusiastically, wouldn't they?

63
00:05:06,267 --> 00:05:06,957
I think so.

64
00:05:07,497 --> 00:05:11,457
At first, the tickling would make the criminals laugh so hard...

65
00:05:13,197 --> 00:05:14,467
I'm dying! I gonna die!

66
00:05:14,467 --> 00:05:15,217
Please stop!

67
00:05:15,217 --> 00:05:17,377
That's a peculiar way to torture someone.

68
00:05:17,377 --> 00:05:19,457
It certainly is.

69
00:05:19,457 --> 00:05:25,497
The sheep would keep going, and as they licked their feet...

70
00:05:25,497 --> 00:05:26,847
What do you think would happen?

71
00:05:27,297 --> 00:05:28,437
What?

72
00:05:28,437 --> 00:05:34,077
Since the criminals would be completely consumed by laughter,
they'd be transformed into good, perpetually smiling people.

73
00:05:34,987 --> 00:05:36,207
All's well that ends well.

74
00:05:38,657 --> 00:05:40,487
Is that a true story?

75
00:05:40,907 --> 00:05:42,857
I made that last part up.

76
00:05:42,857 --> 00:05:44,697
Oh, Lawrence!

77
00:05:45,717 --> 00:05:47,877
But the rest of the story is true.

78
00:05:47,877 --> 00:05:49,877
We'll never know what goes on in a sheep's mind.

79
00:05:50,267 --> 00:05:56,097
Too true. Whenever I eat dried meat, they always seem desperate to lick my hands.

80
00:05:56,097 --> 00:05:58,587
They're quite gentle, but how should I put it?

81
00:05:58,987 --> 00:06:02,527
They don't understand the concept of moderation, so things can turn a bit scary.

82
00:06:02,527 --> 00:06:06,197
On the other hand, your faithful knight seems to be quite obedient.

83
00:06:06,197 --> 00:06:06,817
Who?

84
00:06:07,497 --> 00:06:12,427
Oh! Well, Enek can be stubborn.
He refuses to listen at times.

85
00:06:13,767 --> 00:06:15,167
Sorry.

86
00:06:19,067 --> 00:06:22,777
He seems quite well-trained, though.

87
00:06:23,197 --> 00:06:26,907
It goes to show how skilled of a shepherdess you are.

88
00:06:27,467 --> 00:06:28,907
Not at all.

89
00:06:33,727 --> 00:06:34,757
What is it, Holo?

90
00:06:34,757 --> 00:06:36,297
Drunk already?

91
00:06:36,297 --> 00:06:37,857
Absolutely not.

92
00:06:37,857 --> 00:06:39,597
Sure looks that way.

93
00:06:39,597 --> 00:06:41,237
I can't leave you alone for a minute.

94
00:06:42,037 --> 00:06:43,687
As if I'd...

95
00:06:43,687 --> 00:06:45,267
be drunk...

96
00:06:45,267 --> 00:06:48,117
after only three cups...

97
00:06:49,387 --> 00:06:51,037
H-Holo?

98
00:06:53,307 --> 00:06:58,057
<i>Maybe it's because I lived alone in the wheat fields for so many hundreds of years...</i>

99
00:06:58,057 --> 00:07:00,757
<i>The days rolled by so peacefully,</i>

100
00:07:00,757 --> 00:07:02,897
<i>with no distinction between today or the day before it,</i>

101
00:07:02,897 --> 00:07:04,987
<i>or tomorrow, or the days that followed.</i>

102
00:07:05,767 --> 00:07:08,747
<i>And yet, certain days would stand out in my mind:</i>

103
00:07:09,027 --> 00:07:11,457
<i>The Harvest Festival that was held once a year.</i>

104
00:07:11,457 --> 00:07:13,577
<i>The Seed Sowing Festival, twice a year. </i><i></i>

105
00:07:13,877 --> 00:07:16,047
<i>The festival where they would pray away the rain.</i>

106
00:07:16,457 --> 00:07:19,047
<i>The festival where they would pray away the wind.</i>

107
00:07:19,047 --> 00:07:24,357
<i>But the rest of the year was dull and full of mundane, uncelebrated days.</i>

108
00:07:24,357 --> 00:07:28,807
<i>If I let my attention slip, sometimes I might find that what was a seedling had grown into a fine tree.</i>

109
00:07:29,937 --> 00:07:35,947
<i>But now, every day of our travels brings new experiences.</i>

110
00:07:35,947 --> 00:07:40,987
<i>The few months I've spent with this young merchant
are more vivid than all the decades that I spent in the wheat. </i><i></i>

111
00:07:42,767 --> 00:07:45,487
<i>We might have heated arguments in the morning,</i>

112
00:07:45,937 --> 00:07:50,077
<i>only to reconcile by lunch, when I'd tease him as he wiped breadcrumbs from my mouth.</i>

113
00:07:50,677 --> 00:07:53,397
<i>In the evening, we'd get into another fight over our meal,</i>

114
00:07:53,397 --> 00:07:55,837
<i>but at night, we'd speak peacefully of the days to come.</i>

115
00:07:56,867 --> 00:07:59,907
<i>I've traveled with humans several times before,</i>

116
00:08:00,547 --> 00:08:04,087
<i>but now I don't have time to even think of those days.</i>

117
00:08:04,507 --> 00:08:06,597
<i>What did he do yesterday?</i>

118
00:08:06,597 --> 00:08:08,677
<i>How was he this morning?</i>

119
00:08:09,157 --> 00:08:12,177
<i>I'm too busy with these thoughts to consider anything else.</i>

120
00:08:13,077 --> 00:08:17,787
<i>It was only in the early days that I would sometimes remember my homeland and find myself crying.</i>

121
00:08:18,687 --> 00:08:23,257
<i>Thanks to these ever-exciting days,
I have no time to feel down.</i>

122
00:08:23,857 --> 00:08:25,817
<i>To say this journey wasn't fun would be a lie. </i><i></i>

123
00:08:26,367 --> 00:08:28,917
<i>Indeed, I'm a bit worried that I may be having </i>too<i> much fun.</i>

124
00:08:34,187 --> 00:08:35,437
Heavy...

125
00:08:35,977 --> 00:08:37,247
Why am I in the inn?

126
00:08:42,067 --> 00:08:43,977
Wh-What was that?

127
00:08:44,967 --> 00:08:47,287
If that was real, I'll die of shame.

128
00:08:48,087 --> 00:08:49,687
It must've been a nightmare.

129
00:08:50,597 --> 00:08:52,117
I'll pretend it never happened.

130
00:08:54,067 --> 00:08:55,807
I wonder why...

131
00:08:56,667 --> 00:09:00,767
I lower my guard every time he gives me that silly look.

132
00:09:01,247 --> 00:09:04,447
I get angry when I dislike his attitude, and I laugh when he's funny.

133
00:09:05,827 --> 00:09:09,467
I've shamed the title of wise wolf.

134
00:09:14,177 --> 00:09:15,407
Yes, this is better.

135
00:09:16,017 --> 00:09:20,967
This is the position I should take to immediately react to any enemy attacks.

136
00:09:23,867 --> 00:09:26,677
That simpleton, on the other hand...

137
00:09:27,267 --> 00:09:29,687
He's no better than a stupid cat.

138
00:09:29,687 --> 00:09:35,617
Well, maybe being so defenseless and thickheaded is what you need to live in the human world.

139
00:09:40,117 --> 00:09:41,767
Why isn't he here now?

140
00:09:42,467 --> 00:09:44,797
How could he leave me alone at a time like this?

141
00:09:44,797 --> 00:09:46,537
He's so useless.

142
00:09:52,247 --> 00:09:54,277
What's wrong with me?

143
00:10:13,627 --> 00:10:15,777
Oh, you're finally back?

144
00:10:16,387 --> 00:10:18,637
Why did you hide that you're feeling ill?

145
00:10:18,637 --> 00:10:20,437
Well...

146
00:10:20,437 --> 00:10:21,827
You're not a child.

147
00:10:21,827 --> 00:10:24,357
Don't claim you didn't notice before you collapsed.

148
00:10:25,007 --> 00:10:28,737
What would you have done if we were on the road?

149
00:10:29,237 --> 00:10:31,427
You might not know this,

150
00:10:31,427 --> 00:10:34,697
but getting sick in the middle of a forest or the wilderness could mean death.

151
00:10:38,257 --> 00:10:39,997
I'm really worried about you.

152
00:10:40,477 --> 00:10:41,687
Sorry.

153
00:10:41,687 --> 00:10:43,207
So, how do you feel?

154
00:10:44,337 --> 00:10:46,407
It's nothing serious.

155
00:10:46,407 --> 00:10:48,297
I'm just tired.

156
00:10:48,297 --> 00:10:51,487
It's not some strange illness, is it?

157
00:10:52,257 --> 00:10:53,637
Not at all.

158
00:10:53,637 --> 00:10:54,887
Good...

159
00:10:54,887 --> 00:10:55,717
Thank God.

160
00:10:56,257 --> 00:10:57,877
I'm very relieved to hear it.

161
00:10:57,877 --> 00:10:59,847
You're overreacting.

162
00:10:59,847 --> 00:11:04,387
Well, the more I thought about it, the more I worried.

163
00:11:05,557 --> 00:11:08,237
And I thought... that...

164
00:11:08,527 --> 00:11:09,667
What?

165
00:11:09,667 --> 00:11:10,487
Tell me.

166
00:11:10,487 --> 00:11:14,407
Well, I thought the onions you ate might've made you sick.

167
00:11:17,427 --> 00:11:19,247
I'm not a dog.

168
00:11:19,247 --> 00:11:21,157
Of course not.

169
00:11:21,157 --> 00:11:22,627
You're the wise wolf!

170
00:11:24,787 --> 00:11:29,667
I'm disappointed that I couldn't enjoy the food and drink, though.

171
00:11:29,667 --> 00:11:31,397
I'm a merchant, you know.

172
00:11:32,157 --> 00:11:33,257
That's a mistake I'd never make.

173
00:11:34,067 --> 00:11:36,447
I asked to take the leftovers with me.

174
00:11:36,447 --> 00:11:37,757
Really?

175
00:11:37,757 --> 00:11:38,837
Of course!

176
00:11:38,837 --> 00:11:40,327
I'll bring them to you now.

177
00:11:41,097 --> 00:11:42,597
I'd like to, anyway.

178
00:11:45,557 --> 00:11:46,857
But you have a fever.

179
00:11:47,647 --> 00:11:49,107
You're completely exhausted, aren't you?

180
00:11:51,317 --> 00:11:52,857
It's your fault!

181
00:11:52,857 --> 00:11:53,637
Huh?

182
00:11:53,637 --> 00:11:58,907
Your fingers are hard, like the tip of a wolf's nose.

183
00:11:59,557 --> 00:12:03,307
And when we wolves rub our noses together, it's like when you humans...

184
00:12:04,917 --> 00:12:05,877
I can't say it.

185
00:12:06,587 --> 00:12:07,547
No, please explain.

186
00:12:07,547 --> 00:12:08,807
It's nothing!

187
00:12:08,807 --> 00:12:09,857
Just forget it!

188
00:12:12,237 --> 00:12:14,187
I'll save your meal for when you feel better.

189
00:12:16,757 --> 00:12:18,007
Sleep well.

190
00:12:40,337 --> 00:12:41,377
Lawrence!

191
00:12:53,207 --> 00:12:54,747
You're so warm...

192
00:13:06,747 --> 00:13:08,257
Holo, are you awake?

193
00:13:08,257 --> 00:13:09,977
Can't you tell?

194
00:13:09,977 --> 00:13:10,897
How do you feel?

195
00:13:10,897 --> 00:13:12,717
I can't sit up.

196
00:13:12,717 --> 00:13:14,137
Honestly?

197
00:13:14,137 --> 00:13:18,477
It's certainly true that you look as pale as a sheltered princess.

198
00:13:18,477 --> 00:13:20,507
I'm starving.

199
00:13:20,507 --> 00:13:22,157
That's a good sign.

200
00:13:22,867 --> 00:13:24,687
Then I'll get some porridge for you.

201
00:13:25,417 --> 00:13:27,287
I'm thirsty too.

202
00:13:28,017 --> 00:13:29,407
Is that water?

203
00:13:29,407 --> 00:13:31,057
Yes... well, no.

204
00:13:31,057 --> 00:13:33,577
Since you had a fever last night, I bought apple cider.

205
00:13:33,577 --> 00:13:34,317
Really?!

206
00:13:35,297 --> 00:13:37,497
H-Hey, are you all right?

207
00:13:40,297 --> 00:13:43,927
If only you were usually even half this peaceable.

208
00:13:43,927 --> 00:13:47,017
When I sleep peacefully in the wagon,
you get angry with me.

209
00:13:47,687 --> 00:13:50,267
Well, it isn't fair if I'm the only one awake, now is it?

210
00:13:50,907 --> 00:13:55,477
And if I were always a meek little lamb,
you'd eat my share of the food.

211
00:13:55,477 --> 00:13:57,487
I'm a grown man. I need to eat more than you.

212
00:13:57,487 --> 00:14:00,797
Then, to counter that, I should have an even bigger attitude.

213
00:14:05,717 --> 00:14:06,767
What is it?

214
00:14:06,767 --> 00:14:08,367
It doesn't taste right...

215
00:14:08,367 --> 00:14:10,127
Oh, that's because I diluted it.

216
00:14:10,587 --> 00:14:12,167
Too much!

217
00:14:12,167 --> 00:14:14,587
I thought my nose had gone stupid.

218
00:14:15,557 --> 00:14:17,467
Diluted apple cider is good for fevers.

219
00:14:19,387 --> 00:14:21,477
Didn't you know that?

220
00:14:21,967 --> 00:14:23,977
I am the wise wolf,

221
00:14:23,977 --> 00:14:28,937
so I know enough about the world to know there are things I don't know.

222
00:14:29,737 --> 00:14:33,947
Diseases develop when the body's equilibrium is upset,

223
00:14:33,947 --> 00:14:35,687
like the ebb and flow of the tide.

224
00:14:36,367 --> 00:14:41,977
So when that happens, you restore the body's balance by regulating its four states.

225
00:14:41,977 --> 00:14:43,357
Four states?

226
00:14:43,357 --> 00:14:44,197
Yes!

227
00:14:44,197 --> 00:14:48,207
Hot and cold, dry and wet. These are the four states.

228
00:14:49,037 --> 00:14:50,467
All right. And?

229
00:14:50,467 --> 00:14:52,917
You can regulate them by what you choose to eat or drink.

230
00:14:53,767 --> 00:14:57,297
You had a fever, so you needed something cold.

231
00:14:57,297 --> 00:14:59,437
That's why I brought you apple cider!

232
00:14:59,437 --> 00:15:02,487
I would rather eat a real apple.

233
00:15:02,487 --> 00:15:03,967
That wouldn't help.

234
00:15:04,897 --> 00:15:07,967
Apples are cold, yes, but they're dry.

235
00:15:07,967 --> 00:15:12,057
Sick people are too dry, so you need to get them wet foods.

236
00:15:12,057 --> 00:15:14,627
Which means you needed something to drink.

237
00:15:14,627 --> 00:15:17,307
Even having alcohol would make you feel better,

238
00:15:17,307 --> 00:15:21,447
but strong drinks are hot, so they should be diluted.

239
00:15:21,447 --> 00:15:25,727
So, what else are you planning to make me eat?

240
00:15:25,727 --> 00:15:26,777
Good question.

241
00:15:26,777 --> 00:15:31,497
When fatigue causes a fever, you need to cool your body down first.

242
00:15:31,497 --> 00:15:35,377
But since your body's too dry,
you need to get it wet again.

243
00:15:35,977 --> 00:15:40,737
But that cools you down further, and if people get too cold they get depressed.

244
00:15:41,847 --> 00:15:45,147
So, how about wheat porridge with hot sheep's milk,

245
00:15:45,147 --> 00:15:49,447
mixed with apple slices and goat's cheese?

246
00:15:50,637 --> 00:15:52,057
Sounds perfect!

247
00:15:52,057 --> 00:15:53,147
I'd love that!

248
00:15:54,197 --> 00:15:55,647
Hey now...

249
00:15:55,647 --> 00:15:58,507
Are you sure you haven't recovered already?

250
00:15:58,507 --> 00:16:00,637
I-I feel dizzy...

251
00:16:00,057 --> 00:16:00,647
Holo!

252
00:16:01,627 --> 00:16:02,797
Hurry with that food, okay?

253
00:16:04,967 --> 00:16:05,987
I'll be right back.

254
00:16:05,987 --> 00:16:07,737
I want a big bowl!

255
00:16:30,977 --> 00:16:32,627
Lawrence!

256
00:16:53,847 --> 00:16:54,867
Can you sit up?

257
00:16:55,657 --> 00:16:56,367
No...

258
00:17:00,117 --> 00:17:04,857
I hope you'll take care of me when I'm sick.

259
00:17:04,857 --> 00:17:07,717
I will, if you don't mind treatment the way it's done in Yoitsu.

260
00:17:08,197 --> 00:17:09,487
I'll think about it.

261
00:17:10,897 --> 00:17:14,577
Worry not, I have a good nose.

262
00:17:14,577 --> 00:17:18,027
I'll take action before you even get sick.

263
00:17:19,177 --> 00:17:21,577
I'm sorry for not noticing,

264
00:17:21,577 --> 00:17:24,397
but you should've told me.

265
00:17:25,067 --> 00:17:27,237
You know how thickheaded I'm supposed to be.

266
00:17:28,987 --> 00:17:33,497
I might have a much more serious disease,
but I'm sure you'd fail to notice in any case.

267
00:17:34,767 --> 00:17:36,207
Nothing.

268
00:17:36,707 --> 00:17:37,707
Give me the food!

269
00:17:39,527 --> 00:17:41,457
If you can eat all this, you'll be fine.

270
00:17:42,317 --> 00:17:44,137
You'll be fully recovered in two days at most.

271
00:17:45,567 --> 00:17:48,607
Once we leave this town, we'll be stuck in the wagon for quite a while.

272
00:17:50,837 --> 00:17:51,677
I'm sorry.

273
00:17:52,607 --> 00:17:54,267
Take your time to recover.

274
00:17:55,167 --> 00:17:58,267
I gave up on swift travel when I picked you up.

275
00:17:58,877 --> 00:18:02,177
And since I compensated and apologized to everyone I begged money from,

276
00:18:02,177 --> 00:18:05,857
my reputation in this city's recovered.
In fact, it's improved.

277
00:18:05,857 --> 00:18:09,547
When you consider that, I don't mind being delayed two or three days.

278
00:18:10,677 --> 00:18:13,487
Plus, there are many places I'd still like to visit in this town.

279
00:18:13,487 --> 00:18:14,777
Don't leaveâ

280
00:18:14,777 --> 00:18:15,747
I mean, you know...

281
00:18:15,747 --> 00:18:18,957
The inn is too quiet.

282
00:18:20,687 --> 00:18:22,377
You're right.

283
00:18:22,897 --> 00:18:28,407
We should talk about everything you'd like to feast on tonight.

284
00:18:28,407 --> 00:18:30,007
That's not what...

285
00:18:30,007 --> 00:18:31,757
Do you have any ideas?

286
00:18:32,507 --> 00:18:36,907
Be as detailed as you like, but keep in mind that I need to get to the market before it closes.

287
00:18:39,087 --> 00:18:42,247
You seem better, but anything heavy is still out of the question.

288
00:18:42,247 --> 00:18:43,247
So no meat?

289
00:18:43,247 --> 00:18:44,587
Definitely not.

290
00:18:44,587 --> 00:18:47,357
How about porridge, or soup with bread?

291
00:18:47,837 --> 00:18:49,537
All right, then I want what I just had.

292
00:18:49,537 --> 00:18:51,107
It had sheep's milk, right?

293
00:18:51,107 --> 00:18:53,997
The sweet scent and strong taste were delicious.

294
00:18:53,997 --> 00:18:55,187
It'll do just fine!

295
00:18:55,187 --> 00:18:57,117
Sheep's milk, huh?

296
00:18:57,117 --> 00:18:58,807
Is that a problem?

297
00:18:58,807 --> 00:19:02,357
It can sour quickly, so the price for good milk goes up in the afternoon.

298
00:19:02,927 --> 00:19:04,657
You want it fresh, I assume.

299
00:19:04,657 --> 00:19:05,867
Of course!

300
00:19:05,867 --> 00:19:08,467
Then I should ask Norah to help me find some again.

301
00:19:08,467 --> 00:19:10,307
When it comes to this sort of thing,

302
00:19:10,307 --> 00:19:11,737
shepherds are quiteâ

303
00:19:12,887 --> 00:19:17,457
W-Well, if I ask her, I'll be able to get my hands on the best milk.

304
00:19:17,997 --> 00:19:22,397
If you just spend a bit more, you can get it without relying on her!

305
00:19:22,397 --> 00:19:25,117
Th-That might be true, but...

306
00:19:25,117 --> 00:19:26,397
But what?!

307
00:19:28,717 --> 00:19:29,367
Whâ

308
00:19:30,237 --> 00:19:33,477
Why do you hold a grudge against Norah?

309
00:19:33,477 --> 00:19:34,237
What?

310
00:19:34,917 --> 00:19:36,887
Wh-What did you just say?

311
00:19:36,887 --> 00:19:38,497
W-Well, it's just that...

312
00:19:38,917 --> 00:19:41,867
I don't know what shepherds did to you in the past,

313
00:19:41,867 --> 00:19:45,377
and I can understand that, as a wolf,
you can't bring yourself to like them,

314
00:19:45,827 --> 00:19:48,887
but you don't have to bare your fangs at her so much.

315
00:19:49,497 --> 00:19:53,897
She's good-natured, and there are exceptions to every rule, right?

316
00:19:55,987 --> 00:19:57,117
You...

317
00:19:57,117 --> 00:19:58,767
W-What?

318
00:19:58,767 --> 00:20:00,637
Sorry, I was in the wrong.

319
00:20:01,327 --> 00:20:02,627
So it's okay, then?

320
00:20:02,627 --> 00:20:05,047
Well, how should I put it?

321
00:20:05,897 --> 00:20:07,707
I suppose it's all right.

322
00:20:07,707 --> 00:20:09,527
It's just who you are.

323
00:20:09,527 --> 00:20:10,197
What?

324
00:20:10,197 --> 00:20:11,897
I'm looking forward to the sheep's milk.

325
00:20:35,527 --> 00:20:37,747
Norah came to see you.

326
00:20:37,747 --> 00:20:39,637
How are you feeling?

327
00:20:40,677 --> 00:20:45,187
Oh, just fine. It's just a bit of fatigue.

328
00:20:47,607 --> 00:20:51,567
But I wouldn't mind talking with you for a bit.

329
00:20:52,317 --> 00:20:55,777
There's something I've always wanted to ask you.

330
00:20:56,407 --> 00:20:57,577
Go ahead.

331
00:20:57,577 --> 00:20:59,547
I hope I have the answer.

332
00:21:00,117 --> 00:21:02,907
What is the trick to leading sheep?

333
00:21:13,747 --> 00:21:15,717
A generous and patient heart.

334
00:21:30,887 --> 00:21:32,577
The wind's picking up.

335
00:21:34,127 --> 00:21:35,317
You're right, surely.

336
00:21:36,257 --> 00:21:39,947
Sheep always think they're so clever, after all.

337
00:21:40,847 --> 00:21:42,447
Talking about something interesting?

338
00:21:42,887 --> 00:21:44,697
N-No...

339
00:21:46,017 --> 00:21:47,967
It's our little secret. Just us girls.

340
00:21:49,267 --> 00:21:50,317
Oh, I see...

